===Etymology===
{{root|en|ar|ء ل ه|ك ب ر}}
From {{bor|en|ar|اللّٰهُ أَكْبَر}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˌaləhuː ˈakbɑː/|/əˌlɑːhuː ˈakbɑː/}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interj|nolinkhead=1}}

# {{lb|en|Islam}} "[[God]] is most [[great]]"; the [[Muslim]] [[takbir]], or liturgical [[proclamation]], used to accept sovereignty of God and as a common form of thanksgiving. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1742|author=James Fraser|title=The History of Nadir Shah|location=London|page=126n
|passage=''Tokbîr'' is repeating three times these Words, {{lang|ar|اللّٰه أكبر}} '''''Allah Akbar''''', which is to say, GOD ''is Greatest'', before the ''Kelmah'' abovementioned.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Yuri Bagrov|journal=The Guardian|date=22 Jun 2004
|passage=The official said it was not immediately clear who the attackers were, but said some of them were shouting "'''Allahu Akbar'''" - a frequent cry of Chechnya's separatist rebels.}}

